# Who has to approve a change to what. GitHub reads this file from the
# repository root, .github/ or docs/, and the first one it finds is the only
# one it reads.
#
# The outcome tree is here because the merge gate rests on it: an outcome
# test that could be edited without review isn't a gate, it's a suggestion --
# an agent that can't satisfy a failing test can just delete it. Requiring a
# code owner's approval on any diff touching one is what makes "every outcome
# test passes" mean the application was fixed. `seal check` refuses a tree
# this file leaves uncovered; see /docs/guides/merge-gate.md for the half of the
# gate that lives in this repository's branch-protection settings and can't
# be checked from a checkout.

# What Seal itself promises an adopting project, and the tests
# translated from those promises. Owned for the same reason as any other
# specification: a project that could edit what it claims, to agree with
# what it does, is claiming nothing.
/outcomes/ @vdel
/examples/angular-django/outcomes/ @vdel
